Maybe it's because many of us here at CMU are committed to the idea of
freedom of speech, the idea that information on computers is as
important as information on paper, and the idea that we're not willing
to let things of this nature happen quietly.

Within the first couple of days after the memo came out, various CMU folk:
 - contacted the local press
 - contacted the non-local press
 - contacted the EFF & ACLU 
 - started organizing a rally
 - set up a WWW page 
 - got the various staff, faculty and student senates to get together to
work on resolutions against the ban
 - created an internal newsgroup to discuss the situation, and what
anyone can do.
and probably a small pile of other things I'm forgetting (shame on me).

The CMU community hasn't been this worked up about an issue since 1983!!
 I think the tireless efforts of the sleepless Declan McCullagh, Student
Body President and various members of the yet-unnamed-group, comprised
of staff, faculty and students, helping to coordinate efforts deserve a
lot of credit for making sure that this didn't happen quietly.  

The student senate passed a resolution against the ban in two nights. 
The faculty senate is to be commended; as a member said, it usually
takes them hours to quibble about details of an issue, but in 90 minutes
they passed a resolution about the ban.   The staff council meets later
this week to discuss their own resolution.

Sure, the media could have ignored us.  They could have tossed it off as
kids making noise.  But  CMU is a high-caliber institution known
world-wide for producing some of the best computer scientists (and
engineers, and actors, and other folks) in their field.  We don't want
to be known as "CMU, the high-caliber university that censors," and
that's big news.

