>-- Dave Touretzky, Senior Research Scientist, Computer Science Department

>================================================================

>    RESOLUTION PASSED NOVEMBER 10, 1994 BY CMU FACULTY SENATE

>Whereas, this University has a long and proud tradition of protecting
>freedom of expression and intellectual inquiry,

>The Faculty Senate requests that the Administration take no action to
>restrict access to information, or to prevent individuals from obtaining
>and distributing information via electronic networks, unless an advisory
>committee, with appropriate representation of the campus community, and
>after due consultation with recognized experts, finds that failure to take
>action in specific, limited cases would place the University at imminent
>and grave risk of legal liability,

>And further requests that until this committee has issued its first report,
>the policy in effect as of November 7, 1994, which did not permit
>censorship of any electronic bulletin boards, either text or graphics, be
>restored.

    " FREEDOM of Electronic News Groups :- HOW MUCH HOW FAR ?? "



      I welcome your stand to have an advisory committee to look in this 
issue and determine the cases where action is necessary to avoid the risk 
of legal liability but at the same time restore the uncensored access to 
the news groups involved in this issue.
      In my personal opinion I think that the banning of these news groups 
is not right as first of all they are in violation of freedom of expression.
In all universities, the students have usually attained a level of maturity 
that enables them to differentiate between right and wrong. It is therefore 
very important that the decision to decide what is right or wrong for them, 
should entirely lie with the students. If crucial decisions such as these 
will be taken by other regulatory bodies instead of the students, the students 
will never be able to develop the strength to refrain themselves from things 
that they feel are not right and also will inhibit their ability to make tough
decisions in future which in turn will affect their overall personalities.     
      When we move to the subject of the material these news groups contain,
we should not forget that it is these groups, discussions pertaining to 
medical and health aspects of sex life take place that can be sources of
valuable and first hand information of sexual diseases and other related
problems. Also why is it that when students are formally given sex 
education in schools, nobody finds it offending but find the existence of
news groups pertaining to sex very offending.
      On the subject of access of some of these news groups to the minors 
I only want to say that I am sure that the system designers of the university
can certainly come up with a solution which will restrict the access of these
news groups to minors.
      I also would like to say that the groups dealing with binaries, if 
need be can be banned as they are of no great importance but the text groups
should certainly not be banned as eventhough some of the material that they 
contain may be offending to a particular individual, everybody has the right
to express one's point of view in public.   
      I therefore feel that the administration of the university reconsider
their decision and restore the uncensored access to the electronic news 
groups related to this issue. I welcome any comments you have regarding my 
opinion that I have expressed here. 
