I really meant to be done posting on this subject, but realized I'd made an 
error in what was to be my final post.  It is true that the original poster 
has yet to answer the question whether he is a parent, and that other 
responses have included a vociferous teenager who seems not to be a parent, 
and then the latest response from Andrew, who doesn't say either whether he is 
a parent.  These are all from men, as I alleged, and possibly all from 
non-parents, as I alleged.  I did however forget the one poster who indicated 
that he was the father of two.  This was a particularly egregious error 
because I was responding to a post in which he was quoted.

I'm really not interested in carrying this further, though I did want to "own 
up" to my mistake here.  I also want to point out that this parent, though 
responding very negatively to me, seems to take a point of view with which I 
am in agreement.  He indicates that his children had seen television, although 
he exercised significant control over what they watch and how much, at least 
at home.  I have no problem with that; I exercise control over what my child 
watches, too. 

I would just like to say that my response was to a posting that 
seemed to preach a kind of absolutism that I can not buy, and that I think is 
a dangerous approach to social problems.  I did not support censorship--I said 
that very clearly in my first posting.  I did not support "lazy parenting" 
that just says oh, well, there's nothing I can do, or "permissive" parenting 
that allows the child to run the show--and I don't think there is anything in 
any of my postings to suggest that I do support any of those things.  All I 
argued for was a degree of moderation in pronouncing guilt on others, a degree 
of recognition that things aren't always cut and dried.  Somehow, that 
elicited a number of angry postings telling me off, telling me that anyone who 
thinks the way these respondents presume (incorrectly) that I think must ipso 
facto be a bad parent whose child should be removed from her care, etc., etc.  
None of these replies would deign to recognize the very real difference money 
and time can make in how parents are able to parent their children.  So far as 
these people are concerned, there is only one way to look at the thing, only 
one conclusion to draw, and a serious moral defect in anyone who doesn't see 
it their way and act accordingly.

So far as I am concerned, the big social problem we need to face is not any of 
the things they point fingers at, but rather them, standing there pointing 
their fingers.

Flame on.  I've had my say.

Jean


