No one, at least not I, is advocating presentation of illegal acts or the 
implications thereof; however, I don't believe that what is depicted on 
the children's show in question is depicting or suggesting any illegal acts.

> No parent is free to beat their child.  

Again, this deals with personal safety.  Not freedom of speech.  

> The government puts a limit on what a parent can do.  That is a limit on 
> parental rights.  The government has laws that you must send your child 
> to school.  Is that an infringment on parental rights?

Of course not.  Again--my rights end at the point at which they infringe 
on another person's.
To quote another respondent:
	Does my writing a book infringe on your rights?  NO
        Does my presenting a program which you are not required to view
        infringe on your rights?  NO
	Does my choice of reading material infringe upon your rights?  NO
Do I have to concede my rights to a majority?  In a civilized society, YES--
look at recent smoking bans in public places.  (I by the way, am not a 
smoker anyway).
However, a minority, unless representing the majority, does not, or at least 
in the opinion, should not, have the right to limit my access to any 
material I choose--within the confines of what is legally (which is 
a whole other argument) available.  Unfortunately, this is exactly what 
happens, because all too often, the majority is too apathetic to get 
involved and speak out.

